    <span class="cat-badge">Guide &middot; Rentals</span>
    <h1>Hanoi Motorbike Rental: Photography Travelers Rainy-Day Planning</h1>
    <p>Rain in Hanoi is not an interruption to a photography trip; for much of the year it is a scheduled co-star. The city's wet months bring downpours that arrive with theatrical suddenness, flood the low corners of the streets for an hour, and then leave behind the most photogenic light of the whole day. A rider with an expensive camera and a rented machine needs a plan for all three of those phases, because the same water that doubles the neon on wet asphalt also ruins electronics and reduces a scooter's traction to something requiring real respect. Rainy-day planning, for this audience, is the craft of keeping both the gear and the rider on the right side of that trade.</p>
    <p>This is the rainy-day chapter of the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental/">Hanoi motorbike rental</a> cluster written for photography travelers. Rental prices are on the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental-cost/">cost hub</a>, general questions on the <a href="/faq/">FAQ</a> page, and the sibling chapters that feed into this one are the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental/photography-travelers-route-planning/">route planning guide</a>, the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental/photography-travelers-bike-choice/">bike choice guide</a> and the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental/photography-travelers-pre-ride-inspection/">pre-ride inspection guide</a>, because a wet plan only works on top of a sound machine and a sensible route.</p>

    <nav class="toc" aria-label="Table of contents">
      <p>On this page</p>
      <ol>
        <li><a href="#sec-1">Hanoi's rain, honestly described</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sec-2">The rider's rain kit</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sec-3">Waterproofing the camera bag</a></li>
